{
  "note": "Counted by hand from data/pages/config__latest__developerguide__resource-config-reference.md before the parser was written. The page states each recorded type in a \"Resource Type Value\" field, in two renderings: 610 bullet lines matching '**Resource Type Value:** <value>' and 35 rows of the 34 markdown tables whose second column is 'Resource Type Value'. One bullet gives the value 'NA' (under the group heading `AWS::EC2::VPCGatewayAttachment`), so it names no resource type and is not counted. That leaves 609 + 35 = 644 listed values, of which AWS::ElasticLoadBalancingV2::LoadBalancer is listed twice, once as 'Application Load Balancer' and once as 'Network Load Balancer'. Deduplicated: 643. All 644 listed values resolve with resolveResourceType, so unresolved is empty. The 131 '## ' headings are AWS services, not resource types, and are never counted. Related Resource cells name relationship targets, not recorded types, and are never counted. AWS::ECS::Service carries a footnote reading \"This service currently only support the new Amazon Resource Name (ARN) format\", with the old ARN format printed as \"Old (not supported)\", so it is partial, not full. The remaining ten footnoted types are full: their footnotes add context or carve out other resource types inside a stack or pipeline, not the footnoted type itself. The resolve rate is not evidence of anything: resource-types.json lists this page as one of its own generation sources, so every listed value is in the universe by construction, and 22 universe entries exist only because this page named them.",
